# interplay_decodewalk_5: Decode walks (pgtest/decodewalk_util.py): each seed is one scenario, interplay_decodewalk_<seed>, whose program the walker builds from the seed alone: batches of execs whose segments are seeded random streams of valid blit, compute, render and info records, several segments an exec and several execs a batch, continued segments sharing an encoder, the valid edges of section 6.13 (a blit decoder carried into a segment of another type, an info segment continued by nothing, a protection envelope, short ranges, trailing bytes, records longer than their commands, records past a segment's length), and at most one malformed record or segment a batch, on the batch's last exec, which faults at its ring offset and commits nothing (docs/testing.md, sections 6.12-6.19). Every span of guest memory the GPU wrote (two output buffers, the indirect command buffers' target, two 8x8 RGBA8Unorm textures over buffers, read back directly and through blits) is compared with the walker's model after each batch.  The all-setup walks (host-independent) run in blocks, map and isolate, and meet the info decoder only where it throws before a reply (in-process a reply aborts the host, section 7.4); some carry a second task on child FIFO 1, whose execs interleave with the first's through barriers. The isolate walks add info replies (host values, so keyed by host): the first reply of each kind is dumped, every later one compared with it, and a reply after a malformed record is compared with the 0xEE the guest wrote there.  Death-last seeds end in one of the decoder deaths of section 7.4 on task 1: an encoder left open at commit, a second compute encoder over a carried one, DispatchThreadgroupsIndirect from buffer ref 0, a render pass with a size and no attachment, an 0x87 naming a nil ref in a concurrent encoder, an indirect dispatch in a run of compute encoders of mixed dispatch types, or (all-setup walks) an info reply. In-process the host dies in the wait labelled [dies]. Every ending but the info reply and the mixed dispatch types is a death inside Metal, so under isolation too the host dies in that wait, and the steps after it (the task's next exec, the task deleted, defined again and walked on) are never reached; the info reply is one the service writes, so under isolation its exec completes.  The indirect dispatch in a run of mixed dispatch types is the host Metal's to end or run: on the M3 Max, Metal at macOS 26.7 ends the host there and 27.0.1 runs it (measured). That seed is keyed by host, and its walk ends at that exec: its stamp, the fault state and every window compared with the model, which the exec's writes update. Where the host dies, none of these is reached.  python3 -m pgtest.decodewalk_util checks the expected files: every cmp equal, every fault offset the one its label names.
